  "id": "Compound::DB00224 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Indinavir is linked to Cyp2d6 through a series of molecular and physiological interactions that involve multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Indinavir may influence Cyp2d6 via indirect mechanisms, primarily through the modulation of other enzymes and pathways. Specifically, Indinavir is associated with Cyp3a7, which participates in Aromatase Activity, and this activity is linked to Cyp2d6. This implies a potential role for Indinavir in modulating Cyp2d6 through the Cyp3a7-Aromatase pathway.\n\nAdditionally, Indinavir is linked to Cyp2d6 through a chain involving Propafenone and Bisoprolol, both of which bind to Cyp2d6. While the direct causal link between Indinavir and Cyp2d6 is not explicitly stated, the shared involvement of Cyp2d6 in the pathways of Propafenone and Bisoprolol suggests a possible indirect relationship. The presence of a common ancestor, Alcohol, further indicates a broader metabolic context that may underlie these interactions.\n\nThe integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of drug-target relationships, where a single molecule can influence multiple enzymes through various biochemical routes. The evidence supports a mechanism where Indinavir may indirectly affect Cyp2d6 through the modulation of other enzymes and their downstream activities, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
